# The 1994 attract mode, replayed: Stage 1.8b's determinism script.
#
# `demo` points at the `'demo'` resource extracted from Glider PRO 1.0.5 -- 1117 records,
# frames 46 to 3414, which is about 114 seconds of somebody flying "Demo House" in 1994. The
# path is written the way somebody standing in the repository root would type it; the tests
# rewrite it, because a path is a fact about a machine and not about the run.
#
# This is the strictest determinism test the harness can run. Every other script here holds a
# key or holds none; this one replays a keystroke log recorded against a build the port is
# trying to reproduce, so a one-frame drift in the frame clock, the input pass or the random
# stream sends the recorded glider somewhere the recording never went.
#
# No `at` lines, and that is load-bearing rather than tidy. During a demo any of player one's
# four game keys aborts the attract mode (Input.c's BUILD_ARCADE_VERSION block sets
# `playing = false`), so a keystroke log here would end the run instead of adding to it. There
# is a test that does exactly that on purpose.
#
# Sound is off. The mix is determinism-tested by duct.script, which is short enough to make a
# failure readable; what this script is for is the input path and the physics under it.
#
# # What the run currently does, and why the numbers are not pinned in the test
#
# The run consumes all 1117 records. The glider dies three times, at frames 1781, 2043 and
# 3417, in rooms 4, 5 and 15. The third death flags the game over three frames after the last
# record at 3414, and the countdown ends the run at frame 3432. It was 573 records and a run
# that ended at frame 1775, in the start room, until the 'bnds' fix (03d0cf0, docs/IMPROVEMENTS.md
# 4.24). docs/IMPROVEMENTS.md 2.18 says why nothing shows the new run is wrong. The harness
# test beside this file holds only the records consumed, from below, and deliberately does not
# assert the frame numbers: they are the port's physics and not 1994's, and a change to the
# physics should fail a fidelity test rather than this one.
#
# frames is past the last record on purpose, so that the run can outlive its stream. It does,
# by 3 frames: frames 3415 to 3417 ask for a key after the last record, which is the read the
# original performed off the end of its allocation and the port counts instead.
house Demo House
seed 0
frames 3500
neighbors 9
players 1
sound off
demo assets/extracted/res/demo/128.bin
